<Shell title="Make your equipment work together." direction="A / Equipment first">
  <section class="frame hero-equipment"><div class="hero-copy"><span class="micro">Open control for remote & off-grid sites</span><h1>Make your<br />equipment<br /><span>work together.</span></h1><p>You built your cottage setup one piece at a time. Origin89 is being built to connect solar, batteries, backup power and sensors from different makers, so you can manage them in one place.</p><div class="actions"><a class="action" href="/next/equipment/">Find your equipment <span>↗</span></a><a class="text-action" href="/next/sample-site/">Explore a sample site <span>↗</span></a></div><p class="hero-footnote">An open-source system / Hardware · Firmware · Apps</p></div><EquipmentScene /></section>
  <section class="frame"><div class="proof-strip"><div><strong>{counts.entries.toLocaleString('en')}</strong><span>equipment entries<br />in our research catalogue</span></div><div><strong>{counts.profiles}</strong><span>communication & I/O<br />profiles researched</span></div><div><strong>Local</strong><span>control architecture<br />built around the site</span></div></div><p class="proof-note">Catalogue entries include series, repeats and passive equipment. Research coverage is separate from validated integration support. <a href="/next/equipment/#status">See the breakdown ↗</a></p><div class="brand-rail"><span class="brand-rail-label">Equipment families in the research catalogue</span><span>Victron</span><span>EPEVER</span><span>EG4</span><span>Pylontech</span><span>Schneider</span></div></section>
  <section class="frame section-pad two-column"><div><span class="micro">Built piece by piece. On your budget.</span><h2>A working setup<br />is worth keeping.</h2><p style="margin-top:25px;max-width:450px;font-size:19px">The solar came first. Then a used inverter, a larger battery bank, a backup generator. You bought what made sense at the time. Making it easier to manage should start with what you have.</p><a class="text-action" href="/next/system/" style="margin-top:24px">See how the system fits <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="reason-list"><div class="reason-row"><span class="micro">01</span><div><h3>Find a path for your existing equipment.</h3><p>Start with exact models and the job you need done. See which connections are researched and what still needs integration work.</p></div></div><div class="reason-row"><span class="micro">02</span><div><h3>Add what you need, as you need it.</h3><p>Begin with the readings that matter. Keep the whole site in view as you add a pump, another sensor or more storage.</p></div></div><div class="reason-row"><span class="micro">03</span><div><h3>Keep control of what you’ve invested in.</h3><p>Local control and an open stack give the system a foundation you can understand, adapt and build on.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="open-band"><div class="frame two-column"><div><span class="micro">Open from the board up</span><h2>Your site has<br />a long life ahead.</h2><p>You should be able to understand the system running it. Our direction is an open stack with inspectable designs, editable software and documented interfaces.</p><a class="text-action" href="/next/open/" style="margin-top:22px">Explore the open stack <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="stack-lines"><a href="/next/open/#hardware"><span>Hardware</span><small>Understand the board ↗</small></a><a href="/next/open/#firmware"><span>Firmware</span><small>Inspect the control ↗</small></a><a href="/next/open/#apps"><span>Apps & interfaces</span><small>Extend the experience ↗</small></a></div></div></section>
